 "posture": "Risk is rotating, not collapsing: the S&P 500 closed Friday −1.82% at 7,580 (3.1% below its Oct 6 high) in an AI-capex-driven tech selloff, with VIX at 19.4 — still the normal 16–20 band but +12.9% on the day. Breadth scores 44.6/100 Neutral: the 8-day breadth MA (0.605) sits in a 200-day downtrend with an active bearish signal and a 30th-percentile reading, putting the model's equity-exposure band at 60–75%. The collector's 75-stock universe confirms a mixed tape — 35/75 names sit above their 50-day average (47%) but 44/75 above the 200-day (59%) — so the uptrend is dented, not broken. Crypto scores 63.2/100 Neutral: BTC is in a bull pullback between its 50- and 200-day averages, about 30% below its one-year high, funding near baseline (~9% annualized) and only 10% of the top-20 coins positive over 30 days. Net read: selective exposure with a defensive tilt into Tuesday's midterms and Friday's payrolls.",
 "sentiment": "Sentiment is defensive and headline-sensitive: fear that AI capex is converting poorly into cash flow is driving rotation out of megacap tech into energy and defensives, while a hawkish Fed hold (~70–86% market-implied odds of a December hike) keeps duration and growth multiples under pressure. Crypto sentiment is similarly fragile after the Binance breach, though spot BTC ETF inflows have continued.",
